\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


A matrix $\Phi$ has restricted-isometry constant at most $\delta_s$ if $(1-\delta_s)\lVert x\rVert_2^2\leq\lVert\Phi x\rVert_2^2\leq(1+\delta_s)\lVert x\rVert_2^2$ for every vector with at most $s$ nonzero coordinates. Compressed sensing recovers structured vectors from measurements $\Phi x$.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
The acceptable isometry constant, measurement count, and sparsity tradeoff are not quantified in the supplied statement.

Source-record limitation: Small isometry constants and few measurements are not quantified, so the required construction regime cannot be recovered.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Can one construct deterministic explicit measurement matrices with small restricted isometry constants when the number of measurements is small, or equivalently when the sparsity level is large? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2010/33}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record partially solved; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-21.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Explicitly build deterministic measurement matrices preserving the lengths of sparse vectors using few measurements.

